    @* #994 · THE PLATE IS THE LAST THING IN THE COLUMN, NOT A LAYER OVER IT.

       It used to be absolutely positioned against .map-page at left 1rem / bottom 5.5rem, which put it on
       top of whatever the left column had grown into. Measured at 1280x720 with the owner's own four-step
       plan open, the plate ran y 465…633 and the Plotting panel y 520…720: 480 x 113 px of the panel — the
       step list and the open editor in it — sat behind an opaque card for the whole of
       StoryBeats.PlateSeconds. A z-index cannot fix that; only geometry can.

       So the plate joins the flow column as its LAST item, and .map-hud — `flex: 1 1 auto` — takes what is
       left instead of all of it. That is #992's own arithmetic, unchanged: "the Plotting panel is the one
       thing allowed to give, because it is the one with a scroll in it." The plate lands on exactly the
       pixels it always landed on (bottom-left, 1rem in, 5.5rem up) and no constant anywhere has to agree
       with its height — the browser measures it, which is the whole point of putting it in the flow.

       It is still a pointer-events:none card whose ✕ re-enables its own, so it can sit inside a
       pointer-events:none column without losing the one control #992 gave it. *@
        @* The plate: same picture, same words, no keyboard stolen and no world stopped. The PANEL is still not
           clickable — a thing you must dismiss is a thing that blocked you — but #992 gives it the one control
           the owner's ruling asks of every surface in this file: "there should not be a pop-up that cannot be
           closed or minimized" (owner ruling 2026-08-24). The ✕ re-enables its own pointer-events and nothing
           else does, so the plate goes on passing every other click through to the room behind it. The great
           port's own plate is what made the case: ArrivalTube.Title(GreatPort) is "🛬 THE LONG WALK IN", it
           carries the longest of the three captions, and it lands bottom-left across the captain's-remote
           button for the whole of StoryBeats.PlateSeconds. *@
        @if (_storyPlate is { } flash)
        {
            var flashCopy = StoryBeatCopy(flash.Beat, flash.Subject);
            @* #997 · the plate is an OverlayShell in its BARE frame: no head row, no body wrapper, and the
               way out is the LAST child — which is exactly the geometry #996 measured and #992 gave it. The
               plate is one flex row of [art][words][✕], so the shell puts nothing between them. *@
            <OverlayShell class="story-plate" aria-live="polite"
                          Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare" Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close"
                          DismissClass="story-plate-close" DismissFace="✕" DismissLabel="Close"
                          DismissTitle="Take it down now — it would go on its own in a few seconds"
                          OnClose="() => Dismiss(CloseStoryPlate)">
                @if (flashCopy.Art is { Length: > 0 })
                {
                    <img class="story-plate-art" src="@flashCopy.Art" alt=""
                         onerror="this.style.display='none'" />
                }
                <div class="story-plate-words">
                    <div class="story-plate-title">@flashCopy.Title</div>
                    <div class="story-plate-caption">@flashCopy.Caption</div>
                </div>
            </OverlayShell>
        }

    @* #1037 · …AND THE NAVIGATION TARGET IS THE FOOT OF THE COLUMN, not a second layout system beside it.

       M25 pinned this panel to the window's own bottom-centre corner (`position: absolute; bottom: 0.75rem`)
       while the Plotting panel above it lives in #992's flex arithmetic. Two positioning systems that were
       never made to coordinate, and the arithmetic did not know the panel was there: measured docked at a
       great port with a plan AND a destination set, `.map-plot`'s own bottom edge landed 12 px inside
       `.map-dest-panel`'s top edge at 1280x720 and 16 px at 390x700, and which of the two a real screen
       painted there was whatever the z-band happened to say — `.map-hud` (chrome+12) over the panel
       (chrome+2), so the edge of the surface the captain was NOT reading hid the top of the one that answers
       "does this course actually get you there".

       Raising the panel's z-index is PROVEN WRONG and must not be retried: `.map-hud` is one stacking
       context, so out-ranking it out-ranks every control it carries — CriticalControlsTests caught
       autopilot-disengage going unreachable at every size (#1035).

       So the panel joins the column, exactly as #994 put the story plate in it: it takes its own measured
       height off the foot of the window and `.map-hud` — `flex: 1 1 auto` — gets what is left. It keeps its
       0.75rem offset from the glass (now the column's own margin) so `.map-dossier-raised`'s 18.25rem
       stack-above offset and CriticalControls' bottom-centre model both stay true, and geometry rather
       than a z-index number decides who owns the pixels. *@
